--- ANSWER ---
No, this has not already been done here. The rename licence in card 110-4 applies a rule the project settled on 2026-09-24. No existing helper already does this PB comparison by uid, and no earlier attempt failed on this build. Nothing below blocks the launch.

**PART A — the direction**

- **A1, settled already (supports the plan, does not block it).** The central question, "LabVIEW renames terminals when they are moved or wired, so identify them by uid and not by name", was decided twice before:
  - Pre-decided 173 (cycle 71, 2026-09-24), `docs/d1-loop12-17-split-plan.md:360-363`: *"Terminals are addressed by TERMINAL UID after their first resolution, never by name again"*. It is built at `tools/stagekit.py:1201-1221` (`match_term_uid`).
  - Review `archive/peer/2026-09-24-c68-m4a-p6b-rename.md:85` predicted this failure: *"name-keyed P6 gates will keep failing on harmless name churn along any edited net"*. `:100-101` measured it: *"The 13/12 name-keyed rows are renames on unchanged terminal uids."* `docs/cycle27-plan.md:3778-3779` records the same thing.

  Brief 110-4 item 1 follows that decision; it does not repeat it. The one new part is limiting the licence to three uids on `#2626`. That limit is a choice made for this card and has no earlier precedent. No blocking slug.
- **A2, refuted already.** None found. Nothing in `archive/peer/` rejects comparing by uid; every relevant record argues for it (c68 §4, PD173).
- **A3, contradicted.** None.
  - Brief item 2 asks for each `#2626` input's "data type string". The files already say this cannot be read: `docs/NAMES.md:477-485` (*"Reading `Coerce Dot?` / `Data Type` for a real terminal therefore needs … a new op"*) and `docs/d1-loop12-17-split-plan.md:1238` (*"no reader for `Terminal.Data Type` … judged, not escalated"*).
  - The recipe cites this and logs the type as "NOT READ" (`tools/recipes/stage_d1_l2b1.py:29`).
  - Item 2 is logging only, with no pass condition, so this is not a contradiction.
- **A4, unread evidence.** Neither the brief nor the recipe cites PD173 or the c68 review. They should have: those are where the reason for the uid keying is written down. It changes nothing in the build, so I am not giving it a blocking slug.

**PART B — the recipe**

- **B1, already built.** No.
  - `stagekit.uid_edges` (`tools/stagekit.py:1224-1235`) and `stagesim.uid_edges` (`tools/stagesim.py:1056-1063`) compare wire edges by uid. The recipe's E1 and D gates already use them.
  - PB compares the computation diff's open sink rows. Those rows are keyed by sink name plus index, as `result_110-3.json:20` notes, citing `stagesim.py:1056-1074`. No uid-keyed version of that comparison exists.
  - So the 3-uid remapping at recipe lines 23 and 75-76 is not duplicating an existing helper.
- **B2, already failed.** Only 110-3's launch 1 (`result_110-3.json:2,19`). That run failed on exactly the name-keyed row this licence covers. Every other gate passed at diff 0: 27/27 ops, D 7 new / 14 lost equal to the simulation, peak 625.1 MB.
- **B3, helper exists.** Partly.
  - `stagekit.match_term_uid` handles uid addressing, but it raises an error on an unwired terminal (`tools/stagekit.py:1214-1216`). The licensed uids 2832, 4160 and 4165 are unwired by definition, so that helper cannot be used here.
  - The recipe's own filter (`not r["wire_uid"]`) is the right one. No slug.
- **B4, already measured.** The rename itself was measured once, in 110-3 (`stage_d1_l2b1.log:516-522`). Nothing earlier covers renames on a Build Array. `docs/frame-loop-wire-graph.md:50,71` shows only the original names, "appended array, element, element, element, array". The general class of renames on moved or wired terminals is already measured (`tools/bench/q_m4a_diffuid.log`, via c68), but not for this node.

